Linux Boot Process

The Linux boot process is the sequence of steps that takes a system from power-on to a login prompt/desktop.

Boot Flow
┌─────────────────────┐
│     Power ON        │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ BIOS / UEFI         │
│ POST + HW Init      │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ Select Boot Device           │
│                              │
│ BIOS → MBR / Boot Sector     │
│ UEFI → EFI System Partition  │
└──────────┬───────────────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ GRUB Bootloader     │
│ grub.cfg            │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ Linux Kernel + initramfs    │
│ vmlinuz + initramfs         │
└──────────┬──────────────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ systemd             │
│ PID 1               │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ systemd Targets     │
│ default.target      │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Start Services      │
│ Network / SSH / etc │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Login Prompt / GUI  │
└─────────────────────┘
1. Power ON

When you press the power button:

CPU starts executing firmware instructions.
The system firmware is either:
BIOS — legacy firmware
UEFI — modern firmware
Hardware initialization begins.
2. BIOS / UEFI and POST
POST — Power-On Self-Test

Firmware checks and initializes important hardware such as:

CPU
RAM
Keyboard
Storage devices
Network/PCI devices
Other required hardware

You can think of this stage as:

Power ON
   ↓
BIOS / UEFI
   ↓
POST
   ↓
Hardware Initialization
BIOS vs UEFI
BIOS	UEFI
Legacy firmware	Modern firmware
Commonly works with MBR	Commonly works with GPT
Limited boot environment	More advanced boot environment
Legacy boot process	EFI executable-based boot
3. Boot Device Selection

After hardware initialization, BIOS/UEFI determines where to boot from.

Possible devices:

HDD / SSD
USB
CD/DVD
Network/PXE
Legacy BIOS

Typically:

BIOS
 ↓
MBR
 ↓
Bootloader
UEFI

Typically:

UEFI
 ↓
EFI System Partition (ESP)
 ↓
GRUB EFI executable

Check disks and partitions:

lsblk

Check filesystem information:

lsblk -f

Check partition table:

fdisk -l
4. GRUB Bootloader

GRUB stands for:

GRand Unified Bootloader

On most RHEL-based Linux systems, GRUB2 is used.

GRUB's responsibilities include:

Displaying the boot menu
Selecting the kernel
Passing kernel parameters
Loading the Linux kernel
Loading the initramfs

Typical GRUB configuration:

/boot/grub2/grub.cfg

On UEFI systems, the EFI-related files are commonly under:

/boot/efi/

Check installed kernels:

ls -lh /boot/vmlinuz*

Check initramfs:

ls -lh /boot/initramfs*
5. Linux Kernel

GRUB loads the Linux kernel into memory.

Typical kernel:

/boot/vmlinuz-<version>

The kernel then:

Initializes CPU
Initializes memory management
Initializes device drivers
Detects hardware
Initializes kernel subsystems
Prepares the system for userspace

The kernel is the core of the Linux operating system.

6. initramfs

Before the real root filesystem can be mounted, Linux may need additional drivers and tools.

This is where initramfs comes in.

Typical file:

/boot/initramfs-<version>.img

It provides a temporary early userspace containing things such as:

Storage drivers
Filesystem drivers
LVM tools
RAID support
Multipath tools
Network/storage initialization components

This is particularly important in enterprise environments where the root filesystem may be located on:

LVM
SAN
Multipath storage
RAID
Encrypted storage
7. systemd — PID 1

After the kernel finishes its initial initialization, it starts the first userspace process.

On modern RHEL/AlmaLinux systems:

systemd

Systemd normally runs as:

PID 1

Check it:

ps -p 1 -f

Example:

UID    PID  PPID  CMD
root     1     0  /usr/lib/systemd/systemd

You can also use:

systemctl status
8. systemd Targets

Systemd uses targets to define system states.

Common targets:

poweroff.target
rescue.target
multi-user.target
graphical.target
reboot.target

For a server, the commonly used target is:

multi-user.target

For a desktop system:

graphical.target

Check the default target:

systemctl get-default

Example:

multi-user.target

Change default target:

systemctl set-default multi-user.target

or:

systemctl set-default graphical.target
9. Services Start

systemd starts required services based on dependencies and the selected target.

Examples:

sshd
NetworkManager
chronyd
firewalld
rsyslog
auditd

Check running services:

systemctl --type=service

Check a specific service:

systemctl status sshd

Check failed services:

systemctl --failed

This is an important command during production boot troubleshooting.

10. Login Prompt

After required services and targets are started, the system provides a login interface.

For a server you may see:

localhost login:

Then you can connect through:

SSH
Console
iLO / iDRAC
VM Console

For example:

ssh user@server
Complete Boot Process

The complete flow can be remembered as:

Power ON
   ↓
BIOS / UEFI
   ↓
POST
   ↓
Select Boot Device
   ↓
MBR / EFI System Partition
   ↓
GRUB2
   ↓
Linux Kernel
   ↓
initramfs
   ↓
Mount Root Filesystem
   ↓
systemd (PID 1)
   ↓
systemd Targets
   ↓
Start Services
   ↓
Network / SSH / Applications
   ↓
Login Prompt
Important Commands for Boot Troubleshooting
Check current kernel
uname -r
Check boot messages
dmesg

Better for the current boot:

journalctl -b
Check previous boot
journalctl -b -1
Check boot time
systemd-analyze

Example:

Startup finished in 8.2s (kernel) + 15.4s (userspace)
Find services causing boot delay
systemd-analyze blame
Check dependency chain
systemd-analyze critical-chain
Check failed services
systemctl --failed
Check default target
systemctl get-default
Check GRUB configuration
grep -v '^#' /etc/default/grub
Production Troubleshooting Flow

If a Linux server does not boot:

             Server Not Booting
                    │
                    ▼
              Check Console
                    │
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
          ▼                   ▼
      BIOS/UEFI OK?       Hardware Error?
          │                   │
          ▼                   ▼
     Check Boot Device    Check Hardware
          │
          ▼
        GRUB?
          │
          ▼
     Kernel Loading?
          │
          ▼
       initramfs?
          │
          ▼
    Root Filesystem?
          │
          ▼
      systemd PID 1?
          │
          ▼
    Failed Services?
          │
          ▼
       Login/SSH

Useful commands after recovery:

journalctl -b
journalctl -p err
systemctl --failed
dmesg -T
systemd-analyze
systemd-analyze blame
RHEL / AlmaLinux Boot Files
Component	Important Location
Kernel	/boot/vmlinuz-*
initramfs	/boot/initramfs-*
GRUB config	/boot/grub2/grub.cfg
UEFI files	/boot/efi/
systemd units	/usr/lib/systemd/system/
Local systemd units	/etc/systemd/system/
Kernel parameters	/etc/default/grub
Boot logs	journalctl
